  "interpretation": [
    "Structure of the data: the largest correlation between two numeric features is 0.00, so the numeric features are almost independent and form one dense cloud.",
    "The segments that appear are the categories of EmploymentType. With every column standardized, the one-hot columns of EmploymentType are the only separated structure: K-Means (k = 4, the highest silhouette, 0.237), DBSCAN (eps = 2.44, 4 clusters, 0.0% noise) and Agglomerative Clustering (Ward, 4 clusters) give the same groups, with an adjusted Rand index with EmploymentType of 1.00, 1.00 and 1.00, and nearly equal means of every numeric variable in every cluster.",
    "Without that column, the 8 numeric variables show no groups: the best silhouette of K-Means is 0.092 (k = 2), against 0.237 when the categories are included. This is what the near independence of the features predicts: when no variable is related to another, there is no shape for the algorithms to find.",
    "Do the segments separate the risk? The employment-type segments have default rates from 9.5% to 13.6% (spread 4.1% points; ROC AUC of the cluster rate as a score 0.538), and the numeric-only clusters have default rates from 11.0% to 12.2%. As a score, the best segmentation reaches a ROC AUC of 0.542, against 0.711 of the supervised champion. The segments are descriptive: they say who the applicants are and carry a small part of the signal (the default rate of the unemployed segment is the highest), but they do not predict default.",
    "Effect of the clustering on the data: nothing changes in the data or in the models. The result is consistent with the finding of the dimensionality analysis, where PCA could not compress the features."
  ],
